ADS-015 — Validation Model

Document ID: ADS-015
Title: Validation Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Validation Model (CVM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Validation Model establishes the principles, rules, processes, and governance required to verify that Canonical Atlas Objects conform to their structural, semantic, operational, and governance requirements before they are accepted into or progressed within the Atlas ecosystem.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Validation architecture
Validation categories
Validation lifecycle
Validation rules
Validation outcomes
Validation governance
Validation traceability
Conformance assessment

This specification applies to every Canonical Atlas Object.

3. Canonical Definition

Validation is the governed process of verifying that a Canonical Atlas Object satisfies all applicable structural, semantic, governance, relationship, lifecycle, and policy requirements for its intended purpose.

Validation verifies correctness.

It does not modify the object being validated.

4. Objectives

The Validation Model SHALL:

Ensure object correctness.
Preserve semantic integrity.
Enforce governance.
Support automation.
Support explainability.
Support auditing.
Support interoperability.
Support certification.
5. Validation Principles

Every validation SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Independence

Validation SHALL NOT modify the validated object.

Repeatability

Repeated validation SHALL produce consistent results given identical inputs.

Traceability

Every validation SHALL be recorded.

Explainability

Validation failures SHALL identify the violated rules.

Determinism

Validation logic SHALL be deterministic unless explicitly declared probabilistic.

Governance

Validation SHALL follow approved policies.

6. Validation Categories

Atlas defines eight canonical validation categories.

Category	Purpose
Structural Validation	Object structure
Schema Validation	Schema conformance
Semantic Validation	Engineering meaning
Relationship Validation	Object relationships
Lifecycle Validation	Operational state
Governance Validation	Policies and ownership
Security Validation	Trust and permissions
Context Validation	Engineering applicability

All applicable categories SHALL be evaluated before acceptance.

7. Validation Levels

Validation SHALL occur progressively.

Structure
     │
Schema
     │
Semantics
     │
Relationships
     │
Governance
     │
Security
     │
Context
     │
Conformance

Failure at any mandatory level SHALL prevent successful validation.

8. Validation Rules

Every validation rule SHALL define:

Property	Description
Rule ID	Unique identifier
Rule Name	Human-readable name
Rule Category	Validation category
Severity	Informational, Warning, Error, Critical
Preconditions	Required conditions
Evaluation Logic	Validation criteria
Failure Message	Standardized explanation

Rules SHALL be independently identifiable and versioned.

9. Validation Outcomes

Atlas defines the following canonical outcomes.

Outcome	Description
Passed	All mandatory rules satisfied
Passed with Warnings	Mandatory rules passed, advisory issues detected
Failed	One or more mandatory rules failed
Deferred	Validation postponed
Not Applicable	Rule does not apply

Validation outcomes SHALL be immutable records.

10. Validation Lifecycle

Every validation SHALL progress through the following lifecycle.

Requested
      │
Prepared
      │
Executed
      │
Evaluated
      │
Reported
      │
Archived

Validation history SHALL remain permanently traceable.

11. Validation Evidence

Every validation SHALL preserve:

Evaluated object
Applied rules
Rule versions
Validation engine
Validation timestamp
Validation outcome
Supporting evidence
Reviewer (if applicable)

Validation evidence SHALL be immutable.

12. Validation Governance

Every validation SHALL define:

Validation authority
Applicable policies
Approval requirements
Certification requirements
Exception handling
Audit requirements

Validation governance SHALL remain separate from object content.

13. Conformance Assessment

An object SHALL be considered conformant only if:

Mandatory rules pass.
Applicable policies are satisfied.
Required relationships are valid.
Lifecycle requirements are met.
Security requirements are satisfied.
Governance requirements are complete.

Conformance SHALL be explicitly recorded.

14. Validation Quality

Every validation process SHALL satisfy:

Accuracy
Repeatability
Determinism
Explainability
Traceability
Completeness
Consistency
Auditability

Quality SHALL be continuously monitored.

15. Validation Events

Validation MAY generate Canonical Events.

Examples:

Validation Requested
Validation Passed
Validation Failed
Validation Deferred
Certification Issued

Validation events SHALL conform to ADS-006 — Canonical Event (CEV).

16. Conformance

A validation implementation conforms if it:

Implements canonical validation categories.
Records validation evidence.
Produces standardized outcomes.
Supports governed rule execution.
Preserves validation history.
Maintains traceability.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted semantic validation.
Predictive validation.
Continuous validation pipelines.
Distributed validation services.
Domain-specific validation profiles.
Formal verification integration.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical validation semantics.

